      </div>

      <p>For tall users, the back height jump from 22" to 25.5" is the most impactful change the Plus makes. At 22", the standard Leap's backrest ends well below the shoulder blades of a 6'3"+ user. At 25.5", the Plus makes contact where it matters. Seat height, by contrast, moves the <em>wrong</em> way as standard: the Leap V2 adjusts 15.5"–20.5" while a standard-cylinder Leap Plus stops at 19.5". The Plus only becomes the taller chair when the optional 5" cylinder is ordered, which shifts it to 17.5"–22.5" — and that is the configuration that covers users who could not fit the standard Leap at all.</p>

      <h3>Setup Numbers at 6'4" (32" Inseam)</h3>
      <p>Worked from the published specifications rather than from time in the chair — the Gesture is the only chair reviewed on this site first-hand. A Leap Plus with the optional 5" cylinder, set at its 22.5" maximum against a 30" desk, leaves roughly 2.5" of armrest clearance above the desk surface in typing position — sufficient, with room to spare. Its 19.75" maximum seat depth is enough for a clean 2-finger clearance behind the knee at a typical 6'4" thigh length. Those are the two measurements that come up short on every other chair evaluated for this height range. Ordered with the standard cylinder, the same chair tops out at 19.5" and the desk-clearance arithmetic no longer works — the cylinder is the whole difference.</p>

      <h3>The Seat Depth Argument: Why I Almost Chose the Leap Plus</h3>
      <p>When I was building the case for each chair at 6'4", the Leap Plus's seat depth spec was the single hardest argument to dismiss. The math is direct: at 6'4" with a 32" inseam, a typical seated thigh length (hip to knee) falls in the 19"–19.75" range. The Gesture maxes out at 18.75" — short by 0.25" to 1" for that profile. The Leap Plus reaches 19.75" — clearing the same measurement with a few millimeters of margin. On spec analysis alone, the Leap Plus is the cleaner fit for the full range of 6'4" body proportions, not just for users with above-average thigh length.</p>
      <p>The seat depth differential was the strongest engineering argument the Leap Plus made against the Gesture during my research. A 1-inch advantage in seat depth isn't cosmetic. It's the difference between 2-finger clearance behind the knee (acceptable) and 2.5–3-finger clearance (genuinely comfortable). Over a full workday, that distinction compounds: the seat edge either stays neutral or subtly restricts popliteal circulation in a way you don't notice until you stand up. For users who are buying online without being able to test both chairs in person, the Leap Plus's dimensional margin is the lower-risk default. The spec analysis doesn't leave much ambiguity at this height.</p>
      <p>I ultimately chose the Gesture over the Leap Plus — the 360-degree armrests and multi-device workflow mattered more than the extra seat depth margin for my specific setup, and the 18.75" depth was workable at my proportions. But I'd default to recommending the Leap Plus to most users at 6'4" who haven't verified their thigh length against both specs. The dimensional headroom is real and the risk of choosing wrong is lower.</p>

      <h3>What the Leap Plus Doesn't Offer</h3>
      <p>The Leap Plus has genuine trade-offs worth knowing before spending $1,500+:</p>
      <ul>
        <li><strong>No mesh back option.</strong> Fabric and foam only — tall users who run warm will notice heat retention in extended sessions. The Herman Miller Aeron's breathable Pellicle mesh handles this significantly better. If you work in a warm office or tend to run hot, breathability should weigh in your decision.</li>
        <li><strong>No forward tilt.</strong> Some tall users prefer active sitting with the pelvis tilted slightly forward and the core lightly engaged. The Leap Plus doesn't offer this. The Aeron and some other models do.</li>
        <li><strong>Heavier frame.</strong> The Leap Plus is physically heavier than the Gesture or Aeron — relevant if you frequently reposition the chair or share workspace. It's not a problem for most permanent desk setups, but worth knowing.</li>
      </ul>

      <h3>Who the Leap Plus fits best</h3>
      <p>The Leap Plus is the strongest all-around choice for tall users above 6'4", users who need seat depths greater than 18.75", and users above 300–350 lbs. Ordered with the optional 5" cylinder, its combination of 22.5" maximum seat height, 19.75" maximum seat depth, and 25.5" back height covers the widest range of tall-user body proportions of any chair in this category. Ordered as standard, its 19.5" ceiling makes it the weakest of the three on seat height — above 6'4" that cylinder is the difference between the right chair and the wrong one. For users in the 6'0"–6'3" range with average proportions who don't run warm, the Gesture or Aeron may offer a better feature match — but the Leap Plus works for nearly all tall users.</p>

      <h3>Our verdict</h3>
      <p>The Steelcase Leap Plus is the most dimensionally capable chair for tall users in the market. If your priority is covering the full range of tall-user proportions without worrying about fit failure, the Leap Plus is the safest choice. Its foam upholstery is a genuine trade-off against the Aeron's breathability, but for users who can tolerate it, no chair offers a more complete ergonomic package for tall bodies.</p>
    </section>

